#X obj 60 40 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty eps.i~\ and\ eps~ 24 6 0 22 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 76 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Ensoniq\ EPS\ /\ EPS-16\ PLUS\ /\ ASR-10\ instruments 24 6 0 16 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X obj 60 120 eps.i~ 1;
#X text 190 120 <- click it: the editor, f 64;
#X obj 60 160 dac~;
#X obj 60 206 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty eps.i~ 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 234 eps.i~ is the whole instrument in one object: eps~ \, the editor \, the controller layer \, the Launchkey pads and display \, MIDI in and out. Put it in a patch \, put your Ensoniq instruments into a folder eps beside the patch \, connect the two left outlets to dac~ and play. Click it to open the editor \, see Editor. \; Argument 1 is the MIDI channel (0 or none = 1) \, argument 2 the channel of the pads (0 or none = 10 \, the Drums Channel). Several eps.i~ do not get in each other's way \, each has its own editor and plays its own instrument. \; eps~ is the bare engine inside \, for patches of your own: inlet 2 takes the velocity of a note sent as a float \, inlet 3 raw MIDI bytes from midiin \, and the floats of outlet 3 (MIDI thru) go to midiout. eps~ alone takes the library beside the external \, or the folder given with library. Open eps.i~ to see the wiring., f 64;
#X obj 60 504 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Editor 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 532 The editor is the window of eps.i~. Every change shows up in it \, from the controller \, an instrument change or messages. refresh asks for all values again. \; Rows of 8 below the PATCH band \, titled as in Controls: row k is page k of the controller layer. Each cell: name and label \, a slider and a number box with the value as from a pot (0 to 127) \, the box beside it the value itself. \; PATCH band. name: the instrument that plays. library N: load instrument N of the library. user N: a user slot. reload: the instrument or slot again \, edits dropped. init: all controls back to 0 \, the instrument stays. store to N: chooses the user slot \, save patch stores there and writes the user bank \, see Presets. load bank \, write bank: read or write the bank file without storing. rescan library: read the folder again after adding files. \; MIDI in: the receive channel \, 0 = all. out: the channel of the controller feedback. MIDI thru: see Messages \, thru. library size: how many instruments the folder holds., f 64;
#X obj 60 834 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Launchkey 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 862 Launchkey: the Novation Launchkey Mini MK4 with the custom firmware maltepeter2000/launchkey_firmware \, set up the same for every instrument of bela-workstation (launchkey/README.md there). \; epscc 1: the controller layer. Encoders in mode Custom 1 send CC pages from 20 in rows of 8 on the keys channel \, every change made elsewhere goes back to them on the send channel (txchannel of eps~) \, CC 3 asks for all values. \; epspads 10: the pads in Drum mode (notes 36-51 on channel 10 \, the Drums Channel): top row 36 factory + \, 37 user + \, 38 init (press twice within a second) \, 39-43 own \, bottom row 44 factory - \, 45 user - \, 46 save \, 47-51 own. Own pads of eps~: 39 patch select + \, 47 patch select -. \; Storing asks first: save once shows Store to? and the user slot played or stored last (savearm) \, user + / - choose the slot (saveslot 1 / -1 \, nothing is recalled) \, save again stores there and writes the user bank (save). Any other pad or 10 s cancel (savecancel). Own pads and init after its first press show what they do on the display. \; Pad colours \, sent by epspads itself with noteout on channel 10 (velocity = colour + 32 x mode): factory blue \, user and save green (the user bank) \, init orange (flashing while the second press is awaited) \, save flashing while it asks and white for a moment when stored \, own pads purple \, free pads dark. They go out at load \, on leds \, and when the Launchkey asks for them (CC 3 = 127 on channel 10 \, sent when Drum mode is selected). \; lkdisplay: the display lines (display <title> <value>: the control you turn \, Preset \, User \, Init or Stored with the patch name) as SysEx for the Launchkey's display -> midiout., f 64;
#X obj 60 1340 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Inlet 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1368 1 float: note number \, velocity 100. \; 1 list: note \, velocity and \, if you like \, the MIDI channel. \; 1 anything: a message \, see Messages., f 64;
#X obj 60 1430 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Outlets 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1458 1 signal: left output. \; 2 signal: right output. \; 3 anything: info \, name \, 0-127 and value text for every \;   change \, the instrument that plays \, its name \, the channels. \; 3 loaded model layers wavesamples: an instrument is in. \; 3 display title value: two lines for a display: a control \;   (Cutoff \, -12) \, the instrument (Preset 3 \, CLASSIC_PAD) \, \;   Loading \, a store (Stored \, User 11)., f 64;
#X obj 60 1600 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Presets\ and\ user\ bank 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 1628 The library is every instrument in every file of the folder eps beside the patch (EFE \, IMG \, EDE \, EDA \, GKH \, also images of hard disks and SD cards in Ensoniq format \, HDA \, should work too but are not tested yet) \, in the order of the file names and of the instruments in each disk image. preset N \, presetstep and program changes load from it. An instrument is read in the background: notes go on sounding until it is in \, then it takes over. Your Ensoniq files are not part of eps~ and are never in git. \; The user bank (64 slots) is epsuser-bank.txt in the folder of your patch \, a text file: per slot the file \, the instrument and the controls. eps.i~ reads it at load if it is there \, otherwise the bank beside eps~ is the starting point. save \, userwrite and userread without a file name use this file: store to N in the editor chooses the slot \, save patch stores and writes. Save the patch first \, an unsaved patch has no folder of its own. \; There is one user bank and one library in Pd \, not one per object: two eps.i~ in one patch share them. \; volume and voices belong to the instrument object. An instrument change leaves them alone and they are not stored., f 64;
#X obj 60 1978 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Messages 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2006 name v: set a control \, see Controls. \; name: report it as info. \; n name v: set it from a pot or a MIDI CC \, 0 to 127. \; ninfo name: report it as info. \; cycle name [-1]: the next (previous) value \, round and round. \; dump: every control as info. \; library [dir]: use this folder as the library and read \;   its list. Without dir: eps beside the external. \; rescan: read the library folder again. \; preset n: load library instrument n (from 0) \, controls \;   back to 0. \; presetstep 1 or -1: the next or previous instrument. \; open file [k]: load instrument k (from 0) of any file \, \;   without the library. A name without a path is beside \;   the external. \; presets: list the library as preset n name. \; user n: a user slot \, 0 to 63: its instrument and controls. \; userstep 1 or -1: the next or previous used user slot. \; init: every stored control back to 0 \, the instrument stays. \; reload: the instrument or slot again \, edits dropped. \; store n: the playing instrument and its controls into \;   user slot n \, in memory. Without n: the slot chosen or \;   stored last. \; save: store \, then write the user bank (the pads' save). \; savearm \, saveslot n \, savecancel: the pads' store with a \;   question: show the slot \, move it \, drop it. \; userwrite [file] \, userread [file]: the user bank \, see \;   Presets. \; users: list the used user slots. \; bend f: pitch wheel \, -1 to 1 \, times bend. \; mod f: modulation wheel \, 0 to 1: the wave modulation of \;   wavesamples with source wheel (transwave). \; sustain 0/1: hold pedal. \; channel n: MIDI receive channel \, 0 is all. \; txchannel n: channel of the controller feedback \, 1 to 16. \; thru 0/1: MIDI thru \, on by default. \; status: report instrument \, channels \, library size and \;   sounding voices. \; stop \, reset: all notes off., f 64;
#X obj 60 2644 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty The\ instrument 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 2672 eps~ reads the instruments of the Ensoniq EPS \, EPS-16 PLUS and ASR-10 from their own files: layers \, wavesamples with their samples \, loops and loop modes \, envelopes \, filters \, pan \, patch select and the wave modulation (transwave). It plays them on a model of the ES5505 voice chip of the EPS-16 PLUS \, taken from MAME: interpolation \, the 4-pole filter in its four arrangements \, the 8-bit logarithmic volume \, forward \, backward and bidirectional loops \, the 2/1024 pitch steps. \; What the EPS operating system does (envelopes \, voice allocation \, the filter and volume curves) is rebuilt and still measured against the original: levels \, envelope times and the cutoff scale are first approximations. Not yet there: the instrument's LFOs \, the pitch envelope \, most modulation routings \, glide \, crossfades and the effects of the ES5510. The controls of eps~ are offsets over the instrument \, 0 plays it as stored. \; The output runs at Pd's sample rate \, not at the 29.76 kHz of the EPS-16 PLUS. Up to 32 voices \, 20 by default., f 64;
#X obj 60 2974 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Data\ files 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3002 Building needs no data. To play \, put Ensoniq instruments into the folder eps beside your patch: single files (.efe from EpsLin \, EnDiskEx or Chicken Translator) or whole disk images (.img from a floppy \, an SD card of a ZuluSCSI or BlueSCSI \, .ede and .eda from Giebler's tools \, .gkh). HFE floppy images are converted to IMG first. The EPS \, the EPS-16 PLUS and the ASR-10 share the format \, tested so far with EPS-16 PLUS instruments. \; For eps.i~ two files are enough: eps~ (the external) and eps.i~.pd in a folder on Pd's search path., f 64;
#X obj 60 3176 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty Controls 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3204 In the order of the editor: one heading per row \, the same title as the row there \, page k on the controller is row k. Each line: name (the label in the editor) \, range \, CC number and what it does. Page 1 follows the other instruments: bright \, resonance \, attack \, release \, vibrato \, effect (free for now) \, own (the wave position) and volume., f 64;
#X obj 60 3314 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 1\ \ PERFORM\ \ CC\ 20\ 21\ 22\ 23\ 24\ 26\ 27 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3342 cut (Cutoff) -63..63 \, CC 20: cutoff of filter 1 of every \;   wavesample \, also of sounding notes. 0 leaves the instrument \;   as it is \; cut2 (Filter 2) -63..63 \, CC 21: cutoff of filter 2 (the second \;   low-pass \, or the high-pass in the modes with one) \, also of \;   sounding notes. The EPS has no resonance \, this takes its \;   place on page 1 \; att (Attack) -63..63 \, CC 22: attack: time 1 of the amp \;   envelope \, in steps of the panel's 0-99 times \, for the notes \;   that follow \; rel (Release) -63..63 \, CC 23: release: times 5 and 6 of the \;   amp envelope \, also for notes already held \; vib (Vibrato) 0..127 \, CC 24: vibrato of eps~ itself (not the \;   instrument's LFO) \, up to a semitone \; wave (Wave pos) 0..127 \, CC 26: position of the wave modulation \;   (transwave \, loop position): added to the wavesample's mod \;   source \, as if the wheel were turned up. No effect on \;   wavesamples without wave modulation \; volume (Volume) 0..127 \, CC 27: master volume \, 100 = unity \, not \;   stored, f 64;
#X obj 60 3676 cnv 10 10 10 empty empty PAGE\ 2\ \ PLAY\ \ CC\ 28\ 29\ 30\ 31\ 33\ 34\ 35\ 36 24 6 0 14 #1e3791 #000000 0;
#X text 82 3704 patch (Patch sel) 0..3 \, CC 28: patch select: which layers \;   play \, as the two patch buttons of the EPS \; transpose (Transpose) -24..24 \, CC 29: transposition in \;   semitones \, of the keyboard and the pitch \; tune (Tune) -50..50 \, CC 30: fine tuning in cents \; bend (Bend range) 0..12 \, CC 31: bender range in semitones \; dec (Decay) -63..63 \, CC 33: decay: times 2 to 4 of the amp \;   envelope \, for the notes that follow \; fenv (Filter env) -63..63 \, CC 34: added to the filter envelope \;   amount of every wavesample \, also of sounding notes \; vibrate (Vib rate) 0..127 \, CC 35: rate of the vibrato \, 0.1 to \;   12 Hz \; voices (Voices) 1..32 \, CC 36: polyphony: 20 as on the EPS-16 \;   PLUS \, up to the 32 of the ES5505 \, not stored, f 64;
#X text 82 3948 Credits: Modelled on the Ensoniq EPS-16 PLUS\; ES5505 after MAME's es5506.cpp by Aaron Giles\; file format after ConvertWithMoss by Juergen Mossgraber with help by Claude, f 64;
